-- title:
--   Eₚ₊₁ mod p is non-vanishing at supersingular places
-- statement:
--   Let $p\ge 5$ be a prime, let $N\ge 1$ be an integer with $p\nmid N$, and let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$. Inside the Laurent series field $K((\mathsf q))$ consider the level-$N$ modular function field $\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldC}\ K\ N$, the intermediate field of $K((\mathsf q))/K$ generated by the two series $\bar\jmath(\mathsf q)=\mathtt{jqModC}\ K$ (the reduction of the $q$-expansion of $j$) and $\bar\jmath(\mathsf q^N)=\mathtt{jqNModC}\ K\ N$. The assertion is that there is an element $b$ of this field whose $\mathsf q$-expansion equals $\tilde P\cdot(\theta\bar\jmath)^{-\lfloor (p+1)/2\rfloor}$, where $\tilde P$ is the image in $K((\mathsf q))$ of the power series $1-24\sum_{n\ge 1}\sigma_1(n)\mathsf q^n$ reduced mod $p$ ([`SwdAlgebra.qP`](def/SwdAlgebra.html#L11)), $\theta f=\mathsf q\,df/d\mathsf q$ (`thetaL`), and the exponent is the negative of the integer quotient $((p:\mathbb Z)+1)/2$, so that an inverse power is meant; and that, moreover, for every place $x$ of $\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldC}\ K\ N$ over $K$ (a proper valuation subring containing $K$ and a principal ideal ring) which is affine in the geometric sense, i.e. both generators $\mathtt{jGeomGen}$ and $\mathtt{jNGeomGen}$ lie in its valuation subring, and which lies in $\mathtt{ssPlaces}\ p\ N\ K$, i.e. is rational, affine in that sense, and has $x(\bar\jmath)\in \mathtt{ssJSet}\ p\ K$, one has
--   $$\mathtt{stackOrd}\ N\ \tfrac{p+1}{2}\ b\ x\;=\;\mathtt{placeWidth}\ N\ x\cdot \operatorname{ord}_x(b)+\tfrac{p+1}{2}\bigl(\mathtt{jWidth}(x(\bar\jmath))-1\bigr)\;=\;0,$$
--   where $\mathtt{jWidth}(j)$ is $3$ for $j=0$, $2$ for $j=1728$ and $1$ otherwise, and $\mathtt{placeWidth}$ is $\mathtt{jWidth}(x(\bar\jmath))$ divided by the ramification of $x$ over the $j$-line.
--
--   The element $b$ is the coefficient against $(d\bar\jmath)^{(p+1)/2}$ of the weight-$(p+1)$ mod-$p$ modular form with $q$-expansion $\tilde P$, that is $E_{p+1}\bmod p$ (Katz's $B=\partial A$); the vanishing of its weight-$(p+1)$ stack order at every supersingular place records that $B$ has no zero at a supersingular point of $X_0(N)$ over $\overline{\mathbb F}_p$, equivalently that the Hasse invariant $A$ and $B$ have no common zero. It is used in the construction of the supersingular carrier module and of the Hecke action on it, and in the comparison of mod-$p$ forms with multiples of $\tilde P$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_coe_eq_qP_mul_thetaL_jqModC_zpow_and_stackOrd_eq_zero.lean

